## 1. Introduction
Diabetes is a chronic disease worldwide and has been recognized by the World Health Organization (WHO). Daily blood glucose determination is necessary to monitor the course and therapeutic effect of diabetes mellitus treatment. Compared to the traditional invasive blood sampling, a recently emerging noninvasive approach for determining the presence of a specific biomarker in exhaled breath has attracted increasing attention. This method can measure the glucose concentration by indirectly monitoring the amount of acetone biomarker [1–3]. However, the concentration of the disease biomarker in exhaled breath is extremely low, normally at the ppb level [4,5]. The concentration of acetone in the exhaled breath of diabetic patients is typically higher than $1.8~\mathrm{ppm}$ , while that in healthy people is no more than 0.9 ppm [6,7]. Due to the complex background and the low target concentration in exhaled breath, efficiently and accurately detecting trace amounts of acetone biomarker remains a challenge.
Among the different technologies, semiconductor metal oxide (SMO)-based chemiresistive gas sensors are a powerful candidate for breath biomarker detection [8-10]. The advantages of a high sensitivity, fast dynamic process, small size, and low cost endow SMO nanomaterials with the capacity to quickly recognize trace target gases [11,12]. In particular, the structural, morphological, and compositional dependence of SMO nanomaterials allow the corresponding sensing properties to be easily adjusted to a large extent [13,14]. For composition engineering, extrinsic doping is considered to be an effectively way to enhance the sensing performance [11,15,16]. A reasonably designed doped SMO nanomaterial presents the inherent character of the host material while also exhibiting specific synergistic effects originating from the interaction of the doping agents with all the other species involved. In this case, the electrical and even catalytic properties of SMOs can be effectively tailored, such as the energy band, conductivity, and surface chemisorption [17-19]. However, the method used to prepare the nanostructures can dramatically influence the sensing behavior [20-22]. Achieving reasonable doping patterns in the SMO sensing materials is therefore one of the challenges in developing highperformance gas sensors.
As a new type of functional nanomaterial, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising for building gas sensors [23,24]. Importantly, the porous structure, large surface area, and diverse composition enable such materials to have a flexible structural engineering space. One strategy is to design SMOs derived from MOFs, which have abundant reaction sites and high porosity, for use in potential sensing applications. For example, Zhang and coworkers synthesized porous hierarchical $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ structures using a zeolite imidazole framework (ZIF-67) as precursor; the structures could detect 13 to 200 ppm acetone at $190~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [25]. Koo and coworkers synthesized $\mathrm{Pd}{\mathrm{-}}Z{\mathrm{nO}}/\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hollow spheres through bimetallic ZIFs that encapsulated Pd nanoparticles (NPs), and the obtained sensor exhibited a sensitivity of $69\%$ to 5 ppm acetone at $250^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [26]. Recently, Sun and coworkers synthesized $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n O}$ composites by calcination of bimetallic ZIFs, and the optimal sensor exhibited response values of 6.17 to 10 ppm formaldehyde at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ [27]. However, the studies that have investigated SMO sensing materials that utilize MOF structures as a precursor have mainly focused on fabricating single or multicomponent nanocomposites. To the best of our knowledge, no study has reportedly used such strategy to synthesize doped SMO nanostructures and investigated its sensing applications.
Herein, we propose a facile and low-cost synthesis route for fabricating doped SMO NPs that are derived from MOF precursor templates. The regular and homogeneous arrangement of cobalt and zinc ions in the bimetallic ZIF NPs enabled a more controllable synthesis of the cobalt-doped (Co-doped) ZnO NPs. By simply controlling the Co concentration in the bimetallic ZIF NPs, the structure, surface state, dispensability, ferromagnetism and even the composite of the final oxide NPs could be adjusted. These attributes allowed the Co-doped ZnO NPs to efficiently sense trace amounts of acetone gas. Moreover, the optimized sensor could clearly distinguish between simulated diabetic breath and healthy human breath samples.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ , 2-methylimidazole, methanol $(99.9\%)$ , and ethanol $(99.9\%)$ were purchased from Sinopharm Group, China. All chemicals were used as received without further purification.
