## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 illustrates the synthesis process of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs derived from the MOF structure. First, different bimetallic ZIF NPs were synthesized by connecting the Zn and Co ions via 2- methylimidazole, enabling a uniform dispersion of the Zn and Co ions inside the ZIF structure. When the molar ratio of $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{Z n}\left(\mathsf{y}/\mathbf{x}\right)$ increased, the color of the obtained bimetallic ZIF NPs gradually changed from indigo to dark blue, as indicated in Fig. 1. The Co ions played an important role in the color change of the different bimetallic ZIF NPs. This result is consistent with previous work [28], indicating the successful synthesis of the bimetallic ZIF NPs. Fig. 2a1-d1 show the morphology of the bimetallic ZIF NPs with ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{Z n}}$ molar ratios of $3-10\%$ , respectively. All bimetallic ZIF NPs are clearly well-dispersed, with an average diameter of approximately $65\ \mathrm{nm}$ . After annealing the different bimetallic ZIF NPs under air atmosphere, the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs were formed. The color of the obtained $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs changed to green, and the corresponding color gradually deepened from light green to brown–green with increasing amounts of Co (Fig. 1). In addition, the size of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs increased to approximately $75\ \mathrm{nm}$ , as exhibited in Fig. 2a2-d2.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) analysis was conducted to gain detailed structural information. Taking $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NPs as an example (Fig. 3a), uniform NPs were obtained. From the high-resolution TEM image (Fig. 3b), the lattice distance of $0.248~\mathrm{\nm}$ could be assigned to the hexagonal phase of ZnO (1 0 1). In addition, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mappings were obtained from the scanning TEM images of the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ NPs. As shown in Fig. 3d-f, the uniform distribution of elemental Zn and Co, even after calcination, was confirmed. In addition, to further confirm the homogeneous mixture is statistically relevant, another STEM-EDS image (Fig. S2) included more NPs is also provided and the similar homogenous distribution of all the studied elements can be observed. This indicates that the Co dopant in ZnO was well-formed and distributed in the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs after calcination.
To further confirm the crystal structure of different $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs, X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis (Fig. 4a) was performed. For the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{3\%}$ and $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NPs, XRD peaks of hexagonal ZnO (JCPDS no. 36–1451) could be observed without any other impurity peaks. As was proven in a previous study, the Co ions could be doped into the lattice of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ crystals due to the similar ion radius of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\left(0.74\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ and $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}\left(0.72\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ [31]. The solubility limit of Co ions in the ZnO matrix is largely dependent by the preparation method [32], which was previously demonstrated to be as high as $25\%$ [33]. In our study, when the concentration of Co ions was increased to $7\%$ , additional XRD patterns originating from cubic $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS no. 42-1467) were detected. However, cubic $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was not obvious due to its low content. Upon further enlargement of the main XRD peak region (right part of Fig. 4a), a weak peak from (3 1 1) reflection of cubic $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was observed, and the corresponding intensity was enhanced by increasing the amount of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ from $7\%$ to $10\%$ . According to Bragg’s law, the lattice parameters of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ are estimated and listed in Table S1. As increasing the Co doped concentration, both the lattice parameters of ‘a’ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{c}}.$ show a slightly increased trend, and this is in a good agreement with the previous studies about Co doped $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ materials [34-36]. The small effect on the lattice parameters of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ by Co doping can be assigned to the minor mismatch in radius between
$Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ . These results indicate that $C0^{2+}$ ions can effectively substitute some $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions in the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ matrix when the doping concentration is less than $5\%$ .
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were used to investigate the surface state of the different $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs. The overall XPS spectra is exhibited in Figure S3, which confirms the coexistence of O 1s, $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ , and Co 2p spectra in all of the samples. Fig. 4b shows the high-resolution XPS spectra of O 1s, which could be deconvoluted into four surface oxygen components: $0_{C o}$ for the lattice oxygen of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $0_{Z\mathrm{n}}$ for the lattice oxygen of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , $0_{\vee}$ for the oxygen vacancy, and $0_{\mathsf{a d s}}$ for surface-adsorbed oxygen [37]. Table S2 summarizes the binding energies position of the oxygen components in the different $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ NPs. The corresponding peak positions show some changes due to the different local microenvironments. In addition, the oxygen vacancy ratios are calculated to be $36.4\%$ , $38.1\%$ , $37.9\%$ and $37.8\%$ for the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{3\%}$ to $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{10\%}$ oxide NPs, respectively. The vacancy oxygen ratios were obtained by comparing the integral area of the $0_{\vee}\tt X P S$ peak to that of the whole area of O 1s. As calculated, the amount of oxygen vacancy in $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ was the highest among all the samples.
The high-resolution spectra of $Z{\mathfrak{n}}\ 2{\mathfrak{p}}$ in the different NPs (Fig. 4c) all exhibited two characteristic peaks, corresponding to $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ [26,38], which can be assigned to the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ oxidation state. The XPS peak position gradually moved to a lower binding energy upon increasing the doping amount of Co. Fig. 4d depicts the Co 2p XPS spectra in which the characteristic peaks of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $C0^{2+}$ species were detected. The peak positions are summarized in Table S2. The position of the Co 2p XPS spectra also shifted to a lower energy upon increasing the doping concentration of Co, which is similar to that of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ . Since $C o^{2+}$ can easily lose electrons in chemical reactions, electron transfer occurs due to the thermal excitation of $C o^{2+}$ to the conduction band of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [39]. The $C0^{2+}$ area ratios of the different samples were calculated and were estimated to be $48.8\%$ , $44.7\%$ $46.9\%$ and $48.7\%$ for the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{3\%}$ to $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{10\%}$ NPs, respectively. The relative area ratio of $C0^{2+}$ for the $5\%$ NPs was slightly lower $(44.7\%)$ than for the other samples, indicating that more exposed Co atoms are in the oxidation state of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ in the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}\mathrm{NPs}$ and a lower ratio of $C0^{2+}$ on the surface of the NPs. This indicates that a strong interaction exists between $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , consequently leading to a lower electron density at the Co atom due to interfacial Co-O-Zn bonding [40]. Therefore, the lower ratio of $C o^{2+}$ provides more donor–acceptor reduction sites, which is expected to increase the catalytic activity of the NPs [41,42]. Furthermore, the zeta potential of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs was also investigated (Fig. S4). As shown, the zeta potential values gradually increased with increasing concentrations of Co doping (from 10.6 to $19~\mathrm{mV}$ ). The zeta potential of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was determined to be higher than that of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [43]. In our study, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was indeed present on the surface of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs, as proven by the above XPS results. Thus, the increased zeta potential can be assigned to the higher zeta potential of surface $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ further suggesting the successful doping of Co ions into the ZnO NPs.
In addition, the magnetic properties and dispersibility in solvent were investigated to better understand the physicochemical characteristics of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs. First, the magnetization curves versus magnetic field $(\mathsf{M}-\mathsf{H})$ of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs were measured at room temperature. As exhibited in Fig. 4e, the hysteresis loops were clearly observed, illustrating the ferromagnetism behavior at room temperature. The magnetization intensity and area of the loop increased with increasing doping concentrations of Co ions. Normally, the presence of $C o^{2+}$ ions and surface defects (such as $0_{\mathrm{V}}.$ play critical roles in determining the magnetic response of a Co-doped ZnO system [35,44,45]. As the Co doping content increased, the number of $C o^{2+}$ ions and $0_{\vee}$ defects also increased, leading to an ordered magnetic interaction. In our study, the number of $C0^{2+}$ ions may have played a more important role in the magnetic response, since the concentration of $0_{\vee}$ in $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NPs was previously shown to be higher (Fig. 4b); however, this is not the result that we observed (Fig. 4e). The UV–vis absorption spectrum was used to investigate possible changes in the band gaps and the dispersibility of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs in deionized water. As shown in Fig. 4f, a redshift was observed in the absorption peak upon increasing the concentration of Co doping, indicating a change in the bandgap energy [46]. Because the size effect on the bandgap only can happen when the excitonic Bohr radius of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ is comparable with that of the actual NP size, this effect can be excluded in our case. A previous study revealed that the sp–d exchange between the ZnO host material and the $d$ -electrons originating from the extrinsic ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions may lead to changes in the band structure [47]. This interaction could be clearly observed in our data (as shown in the XPS spectra), which caused a correction in the energy bands in the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs. In addition, the dispersibility of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs was studied by monitoring the UV–vis spectra over time after dispersal in deionized water. As shown, the dispersibility of the different $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs was quite varied even though they had a similar particle size. Among the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs, only the spectra of the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{3\%}$ and $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NPs were overlapping when measuring the UV–vis spectra at 0, 1, and $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , indicating good dispersity in deionized water. This result was further verified in photos of the measured solutions. As shown in Fig. $4{\mathrm{g}}{\mathrm{-i}},$ the $Z n C o_{7\%}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{10\%}}}$ oxide NP solutions became clearer after standing for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , while the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{3\%}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NP solutions were nearly unchanged.
To investigate the ability of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs to chemically sense the exhaled diabetes biomarker, the NP samples were fabricated to gas sensor, and detailed sensing tests of acetone were performed. First, temperature dependence curves were acquired by testing the sensing response of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NP sensors to 5 ppm acetone. As shown in Fig. 5a, all of the sensors showed the same change trend—the response values initially increased with increasing working temperature and then reached a maximum at $370^{\circ}\mathsf C$ ; further increases in the working temperature decreased the response. Among the studied sensors, the $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NP sensor had the highest response value over the entire studied temperature range, with values 3.2, 1.6 and 4.0 times higher than those of $\mathrm{ZnCo}_{3\%},\mathrm{ZnCo}_{7\%},$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{10\%}}}$ at $370^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , respectively. Accordingly, all subsequent sensing tests were performed at $370^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
The dynamic response process of the NP sensors that were cyclically exposed to 0.5–4 ppm acetone concentrations is shown in Fig. 5b. The sensors displayed a fast and steady response when exposed to the target gases, and they could quickly and completely recover back to baseline when placed in ambient air. Consistent with the above sensing results, the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor also exhibited the highest response when exposed to the various concentrations of acetone gas. In addition, the response and recovery times were calculated according to the dynamic response curves shown in Fig. 5b. As shown in Fig. 5c1, the response times decreased with increasing gas concentration; this can arise because low-concentration gas requires a longer time to diffuse to the sensor surface and reach equilibrium. The recovery times exhibited an opposite change trend compared with that of the response times (Fig. 5c2). This is because the sensors require a longer time to recover from a higher response. The $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor always showed the fastest response and recovery times, which were shortened by $20.9\mathrm{-}32\%$ and $4.4\substack{-8.5\%}$ , respectively, compared to the other sensors when exposed to 4 ppm acetone gas.
Fig. 5d shows the response of the oxide NP sensors versus acetone concentration $(0.5{-}20~\mathrm{ppm})$ in logarithmic coordinates. The response values increased linearly with the acetone concentration over the studied range. When $\mathrm{R_{a}}/\mathrm{R_{g}}>1.2$ is considered to be the reliable criterion, the detection limits are estimated to be 600, 170, 380, and 1150 ppb for the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{3\%}$ to $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{10\%}}}$ oxide NP sensors, respectively. To further demonstrate the practical performance of the NP sensors, the selectivity and stability were also assessed. A comparison of some typical acetone gas sensors based on the doped ZnO and MOF derived semiconductor oxides with our $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor is provided in Table S3. As seen, the ZIFderived $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor exhibits promising application in trace acetone monitoring. Note that the Co-MOF-74 derived ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}.$ - $0_{4}$ /graphene sensor showed a higher response and a lower detection limit than our $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor [48]. The response of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}/$ graphene sensor to 1 ppm acetone was 58.1 at the optimal working temperature, and this value was about 34-folds higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ sensor (1.72). That is to say, the response of Co-MOF-74 derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ sensor to 1 ppm acetone is lower than that of our $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NP sensor (6.1). This work provides us a good prospect to build an effective acetone sensor by combining $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide with graphene.
Fig. 5e displays the selectivity of the different NP sensors to some typical exhaled breath biomarkers (5 ppm), including ethyl acetate, methanol, and formaldehyde for lung cancer [49], styrene for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease [50], methylbenzene for hyperglycemia [50], and ammonia for kidney disease [51]. Herein, the biomarker associated with lung disease are chosen because lung is the important organs for gas exchange and expulsion from the body, hyperglycemia is a related disease to diabetes, and kidney disease is one of the most important complications of diabetes [52]. The $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor showed a high response of $18.2\textmd{-}$ 5 ppm acetone, while the response values to the other biomarker gases were less than 5.3 under the same test concentration. To confirm the long-term stability of the oxide NP sensors, the response values were first estimated by continuously testing the acetone response every day for one month, followed by measuring the response values once per month for 4 months (Fig. 5g). All the sensors exhibited an excellent stability with small deviations over the first month (as shown in Fig. 5f) after which the response values began to decline. After 4 months, the response of the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP gas sensor was still $74.2\%$ of the initial value, while the other oxide NP sensors only showed $31.7\%$ of their original response. The above results demonstrate the superiority of the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor compared to the other studied sensors in this work.
Because the $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ NP sensor displayed a good sensing performance toward trace acetone gas, it was further applied to detect the diabetes biomarker in an exhaled breath environment. Exhaled breath samples were taken from 10 healthy volunteers and stored in the Taylor bags. Diabetic breath samples were simulated by injecting 1.8 ppm acetone into the as-collected breath samples. As shown in Fig. 5h, the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor could clearly distinguish between the simulated diabetic breath and the healthy human breath samples. The responses to the simulated diabetic breath were $84\%$ higher than the responses to the healthy human breath samples. Notably, all of the response values were slightly lower than the responses achieved in the experimental conditions due to interference by the other gases present in the exhaled breath, including moisture. These findings illustrate that the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NP sensor is has promising applications in the area of disease diagnosis through exhalation.
The gas sensing mechanism of the most SMO based sensors is explained by the adsorption and desorption of the gas molecules on the surface of the sensing materials. This process causes charge transfer interactions between the surface chemisorbed species and the SMO sensor, and brings the resistance variation of the sensing material [53]. In case of the surface-controlled $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ sensing material [54], the oxygen molecules can be absorbed on the surface of ZnO due to the higher electronegativity of oxygen molecules [55]. Then, the chemisorbed oxygen species including $0_{2}^{-}$ , $0^{-}$ , and $0^{2-}$ form by capturing the electrons from the conduction band of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , leading to the formation of the depleted region and increase the sensor resistance. The reaction can be expressed as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{{\bf O}_{2(\mathrm{ads})}+{\bf e}^{-}{\rightarrow}0_{2}\overline{{{\bf\Lambda}}}_{(\mathrm{ads})}}}\\ {{\mathrm{}}}\\ {{{\bf O}_{2}\overline{{{\bf\Lambda}}}_{(\mathrm{ads})}+{\bf e}^{-}{\rightarrow}20\overline{{{\bf\Lambda}}}_{(\mathrm{ads})}}}\\ {{\mathrm{}}}\\ {{{\bf O}_{\left(\mathrm{ads}\right)}^{-}+{\bf e}^{-}{\rightarrow}0\overline{{{\bf\Lambda}}}_{(\mathrm{ads})}}}\end{array}
$$
Considering the working temperature of the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NP sensors (370 ), $0^{2-}$ species will be the dominant chemical adsorptive states in our case [56]. When exposed the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ sensor to reducing gas (such as acetone), the acetone gas molecules will react with the surface oxygen ions and release the captured electrons to the conduction band, resulting in the reduction of the depleted region. The sensor response to acetone can be described by the following equation:
$$
\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COCH}_{3(g)}~+~{\cal O}^{2-}\L_{(\mathrm{ads)}}\to\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\mathrm{g})~+~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{g})~+~2\mathrm{e}^{-}
$$
Appropriately doping of cobalt is proved to be an effective way to improve the sensing properties of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ gas sensor [57]. In this case, the doping of cobalt neither effects the morphology of $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NPs sample or leads to form of $\mathtt{p-n}$ junctions since no phase of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ can be detected in XRD pattern. However, the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NPs sample does show the highest amount of surface oxygen vacancies as proved by the XPS result. As surface active centers, those oxygen vacancies can effectively adsorb oxygen molecules, which play an important role in the adsorption and desorption processes. In $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NPs sample, lattice doping of Co can be confirmed by the XRD result, which may lead to form many electron donor defects (CoZn ) by the following equation [58]:
$$
\mathrm{Co_{(ions)}+Z n O\rightarrow C o Z n^{\bullet+}O_{V}+e^{-}}
$$
As studied in Fig. 4d, $C0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ coexisted in the $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs samples and the $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NPs sample had the lowest ratio of $C o^{2+}$ , which can provide more donor–acceptor reduction sites [41,42]. With these sites, $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{5\%}$ oxide NPs sample could release additional free electrons and results in formation re of more $0^{2-}$ on the sensor surface through equations (1–3). UPS measurement of different $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs samples were further provided to obtain more information (Fig. S6). Together with the band gap spectra which is derived from the UV–Vis spectra (Fig. S6a), the difference between the conduction band and the Fermi energy level of $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{5y}}$ oxide NPs sample is estimated to be 0.99, 0.92, 1.06, and $1.13\mathrm{eV}$ for 3, 5, 7, and $10\%$ Co doping samples. When Co ions only substituted Zn lattice $(Z\mathrm{nCo}_{3\%}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NPs sample), the Fermi level moves to the conduction band. This may attribute to that the Co doping bring some impurity energy levels, which pulls the Fermi level closer to the conduction band, and finally introduced more free electrons. When excessive doped $\mathtt{\langle Z n C o_{7\%}}$ and $Z\mathrm{nCo}_{10\%}$ oxide NPs sample), the appearance of p-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ may lead to the Fermi level away from the conduction band. Moreover, more electrons can be released to the conduction band when exposed to acetone gas through equations (4), leading to an optimal sensing improvement of $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NP sensor, such as higher response value and fast response and recovery time.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, we present an economic and flexible method for the large-scale fabrication of doped SMO nanomaterials derived from MOFs. Co-doped ZnO NPs were synthesized, which were derived from the controllable bimetallic ZIF NP precursor. Structural characterization revealed that the doping mode, surface status, dispensability, ferromagnetism and catalytic activity of the ZIF-derived $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs could be easily controlled. Among these properties, the rational design of the ZIF-derived $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs could provide more active sites for the target molecule, which is favorable for sensing low concentrations of gases. The optimized $Z_{\mathrm{{nCO}_{5\%}}}$ oxide NP sensor showed a response of 18.2 to 5 ppm acetone, with a low detection limit of $170{\mathrm{ppb}}$ . Additionally, a response time of $60\:s$ , a recovery time of 77 s (1 ppm) and a high selectivity and good stability over 4 months were obtained.
Moreover, the sensor could clearly distinguish between the simulated diabetic breath and healthy human breath samples. The engineered composition and a reasonable fabrication method both contributed to this high-performance sensing device.
In addition, we compared our $Z\mathrm{n_{x}C o_{y}}$ oxide NPs with some typical Co-doped ZnO that was prepared with other methods (Table S4). A comparison of more than 50 studies of Co-doped $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanomaterials and their applications showed that the asdeveloped method for our Co-doped ZnO NP synthesis possesses has the advantages of a simple process, high yield, and strong controllability. Therefore, we expect its further application in the biological and ferromagnetism fields beyond the gas sensing area. Moreover, this strategy can be extended to synthesize other types of extrinsically doped SMO NPs for various applications.
